window.docContents[73].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.PqxdhHooksCommon/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___PqxdhHooksCommon":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E › PqxdhHooksCommon.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 290 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (34)abbrev compress512Nameabbrev toEdwardsNameabbrev compressNameabbrev edNegNameabbrev torsionFreeNameabbrev dsmbNameabbrev scalarModNameabbrev scalarWideNameabbrev kemEncapsNameabbrev kemDecapsNamedef readVecNdef readSha512Statedef sha512StateBytesLEdef chunks128def compress512Hookdef toEdwardsHookdef compressHookdef edNegHookdef torsionFreeHookdef dsmbHookdef scalarModHookdef scalarWideHookdef kemEncapsHookdef kemDecapsHookdef mlkemFromSeedHookdef mlkemPk1Hookdef mlkemPk2Hookdef mlkemEncaps1Hookdef mlkemEncaps2Hookdef mlkemDecapsHookdef mlkemValidatePkHookdef libcruxHmacHookdef spqrHookTabledef pqxdhHookTable\n\n\n\nPQXDH and SPQR V1 Primitive HooksTrusted: the statement. External-call hooks for the PQXDH and SPQR V1 cryptographic primitives (SHA-512 block compression, curve25519-dalek Edwards/scalar operations for XEdDSA, Kyber1024 encapsulation/decapsulation, incremental ML-KEM-768, and libcrux_hmac::hmac), assembled into spqrHookTable and pqxdhHookTable. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\nStub names\n\nabbrev compress512NameL40–41 ↗#sha2::sha512::compress512(state = x0, blocks = x1, n = x2).\n\nabbrev toEdwardsNameL42–43 ↗#MontgomeryPoint::to_edwards(&self = x0, sign = w1) -> Option<EdwardsPoint> (sret x8).\n\nabbrev compressNameL44–45 ↗#EdwardsPoint::compress(&self = x0) -> CompressedEdwardsY (sret x8).\n\nabbrev edNegNameL46–48 ↗#<EdwardsPoint as Neg>::neg(self = x0) -> EdwardsPoint (sret x8).\n\nabbrev torsionFreeNameL49–50 ↗#EdwardsPoint::is_torsion_free(&self = x0) -> bool.\n\nabbrev dsmbNameL51–53 ↗#EdwardsPoint::vartime_double_scalar_mul_basepoint(a = x0, A = x1, b = x2) (sret x8).\n\nabbrev scalarModNameL54–55 ↗#Scalar::from_bytes_mod_order(bytes = x0) -> Scalar (sret x8).\n\nabbrev scalarWideNameL56–57 ↗#Scalar::from_bytes_mod_order_wide(bytes = x0) -> Scalar (sret x8).\n\nabbrev kemEncapsNameL58–59 ↗#mlkem1024::kyber::encapsulate(pk = x0, randomness = x1) -> (ct, ss) (sret x8).\n\nabbrev kemDecapsNameL60–61 ↗#mlkem1024::kyber::decapsulate(sk = x0, ct = x1) -> ss (sret x8).\n\nThe hooks\n\ndef readVecNL65–67 ↗#The n bytes at a, as a vector.\n\ndef readSha512StateL69–72 ↗#The SHA-512 chaining state stored at a: eight native little-endian u64 words (sha2's [u64; 8]).\n\ndef sha512StateBytesLEL74–76 ↗#The 64 bytes of a SHA-512 chaining state as stored (each word little-endian).\n\ndef chunks128L78–81 ↗#The 128-byte blocks of bs (a trailing partial block is dropped; the hook reads exactly 128 n bytes and sha512Pad's output is a multiple of 128, so there is none). As HMAC.chunks64.\n\ndef compress512HookL83–92 ↗#sha2::sha512::compress512(state = x0, blocks.ptr = x1, blocks.len = x2): with st the eight LE words at x0 and bs the 128 · x2 bytes at x1, mem[x0 .. x0+64] := sha512StateBytesLE ((chunks128 bs).foldl F512 st) (all inputs read before writing); pc := lr. As LibsignalHmac.compress256Hook for SHA-256.\n\ndef toEdwardsHookL94–105 ↗#MontgomeryPoint::to_edwards(&u = x0, sign = w1) with Option<EdwardsPoint> at the sret x8: some p = tag 1 (u64) @0 and the 160 bytes of p @8; none = tag 0 @0, the other bytes unchanged. The sign bit is w1 & 1 (tbz w1, #0; callers pass sig[63] >> 7 or 0). The callers test only tag == 1 (ldr x8, [sret]; cmp x8, #1).\n\ndef compressHookL107–111 ↗#EdwardsPoint::compress(&p = x0): mem[x8 .. +32] := compress mem[x0 .. +160]; pc := lr.\n\ndef edNegHookL113–118 ↗#<EdwardsPoint as Neg>::neg(&p = x0): mem[x8 .. +160] := edNeg mem[x0 .. +160]; pc := lr.\n\ndef torsionFreeHookL120–124 ↗#EdwardsPoint::is_torsion_free(&p = x0) -> bool: x0 := 1 if isTorsionFree mem[x0 .. +160], else 0; pc := lr.\n\ndef dsmbHookL126–133 ↗#vartime_double_scalar_mul_basepoint(&a = x0, &A = x1, &b = x2): mem[x8 .. +160] := dsmb mem[x0 .. +32] mem[x1 .. +160] mem[x2 .. +32], the double scalar multiplication a·A + b·B with the basepoint B (XEdDSA passes h, -A, s); pc := lr.\n\ndef scalarModHookL135–140 ↗#Scalar::from_bytes_mod_order(&b = x0): mem[x8 .. +32] := scalarMod mem[x0 .. +32]; pc := lr.\n\ndef scalarWideHookL142–147 ↗#Scalar::from_bytes_mod_order_wide(&b = x0): mem[x8 .. +32] := scalarWide mem[x0 .. +64]; pc := lr.\n\ndef kemEncapsHookL149–157 ↗#kyber::encapsulate(&pk = x0, randomness = x1) with (ct, ss) at the sret x8: the 1568-byte ciphertext @0 and the 32-byte shared secret @0x620 of encaps mem[x0 .. +1568] mem[x1 .. +32]; pc := lr. The caller (initialize_alice_session::<PqxdhRng>) fills the 32 coin bytes from 32 next_u32 calls, keeping the low byte of each (so coin i is tape byte 4 i of that segment).\n\ndef kemDecapsHookL159–165 ↗#kyber::decapsulate(&sk = x0, &ct = x1): mem[x8 .. +32] := decaps mem[x0 .. +3168] mem[x1 .. +1568]; pc := lr.\n\nThe SPQR V1 hooks\n\ndef mlkemFromSeedHookL169–174 ↗#<KeyPairCompressedBytes>::from_seed(&seed = x0) with sret x8: writes the 2400-byte compressed keypair (K.fromSeed mem[x0 .. +64]).2.2 at x8.\n\ndef mlkemPk1HookL176–178 ↗#<KeyPairCompressedBytes>::pk1(&kp = x0) -> &[u8; 64]: returns x0 + 2304#64.\n\ndef mlkemPk2HookL180–182 ↗#<KeyPairCompressedBytes>::pk2(&kp = x0) -> &[u8; 1152]: returns x0 + 1152#64.\n\ndef mlkemEncaps1HookL184–205 ↗#mlkem768::incremental::encapsulate1(pk1 = (x0, x1), &rand = x2, es = (x3, x4), ss = (x5, x6)) with sret x8: if es.len < 2080 (x4) or ss.len < 32 (x6), writes Err(Error::InvalidOutputLength) ([1#8, 1#8]) at x8 leaving es and ss untouched; otherwise on some (ct1, es, ss), writes 0#8 :: ct1 at x8, es at x3, and ss at x5; on none, writes 1#8 at x8.\n\ndef mlkemEncaps2HookL207–213 ↗#mlkem768::incremental::encapsulate2(&es = x0, &ek = x1) with sret x8: writes the 128-byte ct2 = K.encaps2 mem[x0 .. +2080] mem[x1 .. +1152] at x8.\n\ndef mlkemDecapsHookL215–222 ↗#mlkem768::incremental::decapsulate_compressed_key(&dk = x0, &ct1 = x1, &ct2 = x2) with sret x8: writes the 32-byte K.decaps mem[x0 .. +2400] mem[x1 .. +960] mem[x2 .. +128] at x8.\n\ndef mlkemValidatePkHookL224–230 ↗#mlkem768::incremental::validate_pk_bytes(hdr = (x0, x1), ek = (x2, x3)): returns 0xff#64 in x0 when K.validPkBytes hdr ek = true, and 0#64 otherwise.\n\ndef libcruxHmacHookL232–260 ↗#libcrux_hmac::hmac(alg = w0, key = (x1, x2), data = (x3, x4), tag_len = (w5, x6)) with sret x8: fails closed (Exception) when alg != Algorithm::Sha256 (w0 != 0#32); panics when key.len() ≥ 2^32 or data.len() ≥ 2^32; otherwise allocates a 32-byte Vec<u8> (cap = 32) at x8 holding HMAC.hmacWith F key data, truncated (Vec::truncate) to min x6 32 when tag_len is Some (w5 & 1 != 0).\n\nThe table\n\ndef spqrHookTableL264–273 ↗#The SPQR V1 rows (8): the contracts' stubs (SpqrHooks), each with its hook above.\n\ndef pqxdhHookTableL275–291 ↗#The PQXDH + SPQR rows of the hook table: the eighteen hooks above. The first rows of cliHookTablePlaced (SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean, as cliPqxdhHookTablePlaced) are these rows under mangled symbol names, with libcruxHmacHookMallocng for libcrux_hmac::hmac.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E/PqxdhHooksCommon.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.PqxdhHooksCommon/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___PqxdhHooksCommon"},"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Semantics/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Semantics":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA › Semantics.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 94 of 285 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (9)def evalSvcstructure Programdef evalInstdef hookAtOff?def externAt?def fetchMeminductive Stepinductive Stepsinductive StepsUntil\n\nARM64 Small-Step Operational Semantics (Arm64.Semantics)* Layer / Role: Arm64 Trusted Computing Base (Role::Spec, definitions only, 0 theorems).* Purpose & Key Exports: Read-only relocated code image (Program), instruction fetch and decode from executable memory (Program.fetchMem) and from the static image (Program.fetch), code-in-memory invariant (Program.CodeLoaded), single-step transition relation (Step), and multi-step closure (Steps). The executable fuel-bounded evaluators (ProofLib.stepState, execN, execUntilPC) and the test-memory builder Program.loadCode are untrusted and live in ProofLib/Exec.lean.* When to read: Read when auditing how Step fetches from s.mem (fetchMem), dispatches externs/overrides hooks (Step.extern), or enforces Memory.KeepsFrozen. For proof stepping lemmas, see ProofLib/Semantics.lean.Instructions are fetched from memory (Program.fetchMem) at an aligned, executable pc. Because executable memory is never writable (canWrite requires ¬x) and every Step preserves frozen bytes (Memory.KeepsFrozen), Program.CodeLoaded is invariant across execution and Program.fetch agrees with Program.fetchMem on loaded states.\n\n\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 43–44): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.execInst\n\ndef evalSvcL46–53 ↗#Apply supervisor-call handler svc while preserving frozen memory (Perm.frozen), tpidrEl0, and symtab, matching architectural user-space supervisor-call invariants.\n\nstructure ProgramL55–71 ↗#A read-only AArch64 code image:* base: load address of code,* code: instruction bytes, with relocations applied,* symbols: defined symbols (demangled names) and their offsets from base,* stubs: extern functions and the offsets of their stubs,* externs: behaviour of extern functions, consulted at stub offsets,* overrides: replacement behaviour for defined functions, consulted at symbol offsets,* svc: supervisor-call (SVC #0) handler bundle, defaulting to Syscall.standard. See Step.extern and Step.exec.\n\n\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 75–86): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.ofSvc, Arm64.Program.withSvc\n\ndef evalInstL88–90 ↗#Execute a single decoded Instr under prog's supervisor-call handler (evalSvc prog.svc).\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 92–122): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.wordToBytesLE, Arm64.Program.ofWords, Arm64.Program.findSymbol?, Arm64.Program.fnOff, Arm64.Program.fnAddr, Arm64.Program.rebase\n\ndef hookAtOff?L124–127 ↗#The hook at offset off: an extern at a stub, or an override at a defined symbol.\n\ndef externAt?L129–131 ↗#The hook, if any, whose entry point is pc.\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 133–149): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.readWordOff, Arm64.Program.fetch\n\ndef fetchMemL151–159 ↗#Fetch and decode the instruction at pc from memory m: the 32-bit little-endian word at pc, which must be 4-byte aligned and executable (Memory.canExec); otherwise none (the real machine faults). At a hooked entry point fetchMem returns none, as fetch does.\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 161–228): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.CodeLoaded, Arm64.Program.hookFree, Arm64.Program.hasWordsOff, Arm64.Program.hasWords, Arm64.Program.hasCode, Arm64.Program.decidableForallLT\n\n\n\ninductive StepL232–268 ↗#Trusted small-step operational semantics relation Step prog s s'.At an extern entry point (prog.externAt? s.pc = some f) exactly one of extern / extern_fault applies (fetchMem returns none there); elsewhere exactly one of exec / halt_unmapped applies, so Step is deterministic.\n\ninductive StepsL270–273 ↗#Reflexive-transitive closure of Step.\n\ninductive StepsUntilL275–283 ↗#Reflexive-transitive execution of prog that stops as soon as stop s holds: no intermediate state prior to the final state satisfies stop.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA/Semantics.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Semantics/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Semantics"}});